artifact.self_contained_problem:
A spherical manifold is a closed Riemannian manifold of constant sectional curvature +1, i.e. a quotient $S^n/\Gamma$ of the round unit sphere by a finite group $\Gamma$ of isometries acting freely. Two closed Riemannian manifolds are called isospectral if the Laplace–Beltrami operators acting on smooth functions have the same eigenvalue spectrum, counted with multiplicity. By a tower of spherical manifolds indexed by a poset $(P,\le)$ we mean a family $\{M_p\}_{p\in P}$ of spherical manifolds together with, for every pair $p\le q$ in $P$, a finite-sheeted Riemannian covering map $M_q\to M_p$, compatible with composition.

Question: Do there exist a poset $P$ and two such towers $\{M_p\}_{p\in P}$ and $\{N_p\}_{p\in P}$ of spherical manifolds such that
\begin{itemize}
\item for every $p\in P$, the manifolds $M_p$ and $N_p$ are isospectral, and
\item there is some $p\in P$ for which $M_p$ and $N_p$ are not isometric (so that the two towers are not isomorphic as towers)?
\end{itemize}
